<description>
Paint alternating square rings from each colored corner marker, clipped to cells uniquely nearest to that marker.

Input structure
- The grid is background except for colored marker pixels in two or more corners.

Rule
- For every cell, compare squared Euclidean distance to each corner marker.
- Cells tied between markers remain background.
- A uniquely owned cell takes its marker color exactly when its Chebyshev distance
  from that marker is even.

Key insight
- The output is not made from lines between corners; it is the set of even
  square-distance rings around each active corner, cut by nearest-marker
  boundaries.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "symmetry/pattern",
  "src": "colored marker pixels placed in two or more grid corners",
  "transform": "fill each cell from its unique nearest marker by squared Euclidean distance; tied cells stay background, and uniquely owned cells take the marker color only on even Chebyshev-distance square rings"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: square, 7-17 rows/cols
  background: 0 (black)
  markers:
    count: 2-3 distinct non-zero colors placed at grid corners
    positions: subset of {TL, TR, BL, BR}; all 4 corners NOT used [observed]
    colors: each marker has a unique non-zero color (1-9)
  output_rule:
    ownership: each cell assigned to its unique nearest marker by squared Euclidean distance; tied cells stay background
    coloring: owned cell gets marker color iff Chebyshev distance from that marker is even (0, 2, 4, ...), else background
    result: concentric square rings around each corner, clipped by Voronoi boundaries

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct input construction (input is trivial -- just place corner markers)
  steps:
    1. pick grid size N x N, N in 7-17
    2. choose 2-3 corners from {TL, TR, BL, BR}
    3. assign each chosen corner a distinct non-zero color from 1-9
    4. place marker pixels at chosen corners on an otherwise all-zero grid
  validation: run solver to produce output; verify output has >=2 distinct colored regions
```
